  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nWell another day of showers/thunderstorms today with thankfully\nless severity with less impressive environmental conditions\navailable to work with. However, the flooding impacts seem to be\nthe main concern going into this afternoon/evening period with\nanother stratiform rain event in the wake of this afternoons\neastward progression of a linear storm feature. A swath of storm\ntotals over the last several days of 5 to 10 inches of rainfall\nacross our western parts of the CWA around Pulaski County in\nIndiana and orientated southeastward down into the Grant, \nBlackford, and Jay Counties in Indiana. A Flash Flood Watch\ncontinues for areas mainly south of US-30 until 11 PM EDT\ntonight as additional rainfall will exacerbate any flooding\nimpacts in these areas. \n\nThe good news is we should see a break in the precipitation\ntonight and through Thursday morning which will help to let\nthings dry out a bit. However, this break will also increase\nchances of misty and foggy conditions overnight with the very\nmoist surface layer from the recent rainfall along with light\nand variable winds across the area. Confidence on the areal\ncoverage is not high at this time but where fog does develop\ncould become dense at least for a short period. \n\nWith the pesky zonal upper level pattern still status quo \nthorough at least the next several days we will have periods of \nincreased chances of showers and storms each day especially in \nthe afternoon to evening periods. Temperatures also looks to \nremain seasonable during this forecast period.",
